  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nShortwave aloft moves overhead today, bringing about the return of \nrain showers. This rain will move into central South Dakota late \nthis afternoon into the early evening before expanding into \nnortheastern South Dakota and western Minnesota tonight. Showers \nwill linger along and east of the James River through at least \nSaturday morning, finally exiting to the east by Saturday afternoon. \nLight rain showers will be the main storm mode with this system, and \nchances for thunder are fairly low (perhaps an intermittent flash of \nlightning or two at best). With fairly light rain rates overall, \nexpectations are overall fairly low in terms of rainfall totals. \nThrough Saturday, areas between the James and Missouri Rivers will \nsee up to a tenth of an inch, while areas along and east of the \nJames River will see between a tenth to a quarter of an inch. Higher \nend amounts for areas under the heaviest showers sit at around half \nan inch (20-30% chance of exceedance over far eastern South Dakota \nas well as over western Minnesota). \n\nAfter rain moves out Saturday, a bit of a break from the rain will \nbe in order for Sunday. Partly cloudy skies will be overhead, but \nmuch of the area will likely see some much-needed sun. The break \nwon't last long however, as early next week, an upper-level trough \nover the western CONUS will bring about the return of widespread \nrain chances. With the latest 24 hours of model guidance, there has \nbeen a notable shift north in the area of highest rainfall totals. \nNow, the best chances to see over an inch of accumulation through \nTuesday are located in North Dakota, leaving a small area of 40-60% \nalong the state line. Chances decrease moving southward. Naturally \nchances for half an inch have also decreased, now ranging from 70% \nin the north (along and north of the US-12 stretch between Aberdeen \nand Mobridge) to 40% over central and parts of northeastern South \nDakota and western Minnesota. Thunderstorms will be possible with \nthis system, and the best chances to see those will be Monday night \ninto early Tuesday morning. There remains some slight signal in \nprobabilistic models for severe weather potential, but that signal \nhas also decrease with the latest forecast cycle. \n\nAs the upper-level trough progresses eastward, a cooler airmass will \ntake hold of the region. There is a bit of disagreement on how the \ndepth of the trough will evolve, so current temperature trends cool \nthe region down slightly less than with previous forecasts. Still \nexpect temperatures to get a slightly below late-September normals \nnext week, with highs in the mid to upper 60s across the board. This \npattern transition will also provide dry conditions across the \nforecast area once showers and storms move out Tuesday.",
